        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why do I get so hot on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Because there is no airflow. Outdoors, running through still air creates a breeze that carries heat away and evaporates sweat. On a treadmill you stay in one place, the air around you warms and fills with moisture, and the room heats up over the session. A fan at chest height and a cooler, drier room fix most of it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where should I put a fan for treadmill running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In front of the treadmill, slightly to one side, raised to chest height on a stand, shelf or bracket and aimed at your torso and face. A fan on the floor blows at your shins. Keep it clear of the console, the step-on area and the motor hood, and plug it into a different outlet from the treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What temperature should a room be for treadmill running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no single correct figure. As a rule of thumb, a room that feels slightly cool when you walk in at rest is usually comfortable once you are running, because your own heat warms it. Humidity matters as much as temperature, so a dry room is easier than a humid one at the same reading.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the built-in fan on a treadmill enough?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not. Console fans are generally small, weak and aimed at a fixed point that may not match where your face and chest are. They are a pleasant extra rather than a cooling solution. A separate fan with a strong throw, raised to chest height, moves far more air across the parts of your body that shed the most heat.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I drink water while running on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Keep water within reach, start the session reasonably hydrated, and slow the belt down before you drink rather than drinking at speed. Thirst is a reasonable guide for most people in ordinary sessions. How much to drink and whether you need electrolytes depend on your health and the session, so ask a doctor or dietitian for long or hot sessions.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>When should I stop a treadmill session because of heat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Stop and cool down for dizziness, nausea, headache, cramps, unusual weakness or cold, clammy skin, all listed in public-health guidance as signs of heat exhaustion. Confusion, hot red skin or fainting can mean heat stroke, which is an emergency: call 911. Use the stop button or safety key rather than stepping off a moving belt.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

